WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.601
Rust encourages explicit error handling
through Result and Option,

2
00:00:03.681 --> 00:00:06.890
but defensive programming depends on using
those types thoughtfully.

3
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.017
The presence of a Result is not a
guarantee of good failure behavior.

4
00:00:10.097 --> 00:00:13.306
The code still needs to decide what the
failure means, who should see it,

5
00:00:13.386 --> 00:00:16.541
whether it is recoverable, and how it
affects the rest of the workflow.

6
00:00:16.621 --> 00:00:19.294
Careless unwrap or expect can turn a
predictable bad input,

7
00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:21.994
missing file, unavailable service,
malformed response,

8
00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:23.464
or optional value into a crash.

9
00:00:23.544 --> 00:00:26.913
That may be acceptable in tests or in
narrowly defined startup validation,

10
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:30.469
but production request handlers,
background jobs, long-running services,

11
00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:33.222
and command-line tools need deliberate
failure behavior.

12
00:00:33.302 --> 00:00:36.083
A predictable problem should not become a
surprising outage.

13
00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:37.874
Panics should have clear boundaries.

14
00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:40.253
If a panic is used to protect an internal
invariant,

15
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.472
the invariant should be documented and
tested.

16
00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:46.135
If a process should fail fast during
startup because required configuration is

17
00:00:46.215 --> 00:00:48.353
missing, that behavior should be
intentional.

18
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:50.305
If a service handles untrusted requests,

19
00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:53.272
panics should not be the normal path for
ordinary invalid input.

20
00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:56.935
Error messages should be useful without
exposing secrets, internal topology,

21
00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:59.635
tokens, sensitive configuration, private
identifiers,

22
00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:01.426
or excessive implementation detail.

23
00:01:01.506 --> 00:01:03.592
User-facing errors, internal logs,
metrics,

24
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:05.596
and alerts each have different audiences.

25
00:01:05.676 --> 00:01:09.419
Defensive code chooses what to reveal at
each layer rather than dumping raw context

26
00:01:09.499 --> 00:01:10.088
everywhere.

27
00:01:10.168 --> 00:01:11.932
Fail-safe behavior requires choices.

28
00:01:12.012 --> 00:01:14.044
Some failures should be retried with
limits.

29
00:01:14.124 --> 00:01:15.247
Some should be rejected.

30
00:01:15.327 --> 00:01:16.450
Some should fail closed.

31
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.974
Some should terminate startup.

32
00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:19.818
Some should be escalated to operators.

33
00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:22.518
Defensive Rust makes those decisions
visible, testable,

34
00:01:22.598 --> 00:01:25.860
and aligned with the security and
reliability needs of the application.
